<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Tryavna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1190">1190</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uprising-of-asen-and-peter">Uprising of Asen and Peter</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Tryavna was fought in 1190 in a pass of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a> near Tryavna, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. The Bulgarian leaders Asen and Peter ambushed the army of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a> as it withdrew from a failed siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, and the emperor barely escaped. The defeat ended Byzantine attempts to crush the Bulgarian revolt and secured the restored Bulgarian state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tryavna.jpg" alt="Battle of Tryavna" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kandi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Tryavna_Pass.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Rebellion of Asen and Peter had begun in 1185. After his second campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> and a fruitless siege of Lovech in 1187, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a> was forced into a truce that in effect recognised Bulgarian independence, and both sides kept it until 1189. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> used the time to organise their administration and army. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> reached Bulgarian lands at Niš, Asen and Peter offered Frederick I Barbarossa a force of 40,000 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, but relations between the crusaders and the Byzantines eased and the offer was set aside.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In a third campaign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> again crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a>, feinting towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomorie">Pomorie</a> on the coast before turning west through the Rishki Pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> and on to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, while their fleet sailed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to block Cuman auxiliaries. Asen led the defence of Tarnovo, and the siege failed. Byzantine morale was low after heavy losses and with the soldiers' pay in arrears. Asen sent an agent posing as a deserter, who told Isaac that a huge Cuman army had crossed the Danube, and the emperor ordered a retreat through the nearest pass.</p><p>Guessing that it would be the Tryavna Pass, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> reached it first and laid an ambush on the heights of a narrow gorge. As the Byzantine vanguard engaged the Bulgarian centre, troops on the heights showered the column with rocks and arrows; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> broke and fled, and the Bulgarians charged. Isaac's guards had to cut a path for him. The Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niketas-choniates">Niketas Choniates</a>, the principal contemporary Byzantine source, wrote that only the emperor escaped and most of the others perished.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> captured the imperial treasure, including the emperors' golden helmet, the crown and the Imperial Cross, a solid gold reliquary holding a piece of the Holy Cross, which a Byzantine cleric had thrown into a river. These trophies were later carried through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> on official occasions. After the victory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-i">Ivan Asen I</a> was proclaimed emperor, while Peter kept his title and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>. In the next two years Asen took lands to the west and south-west, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> and Niš, and his troops looted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Tryavna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Driven north of the Danube in 1186, the brothers returned with Cuman allies and repeatedly defeated Byzantine armies, most notably ambushing Isaac II in the <b>Tryavna pass</b> in 1190.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
